Polymorphism and Abstraction

Part 1

1.
- The PolymorphismDemo program demonstrates polymorphism using electronic devices. It has a parent class called Device and two child classes called Television and Speaker. Each class has a performTask() method, but the child classes provide their own implementation. The television displays a favorite movie, while the speaker plays favorite music.

How Polymorphism Works in the Code
Parent Class: Device
Child Classes: Television and Speaker
Overridden Method: performTask()
The child classes override the performTask() method from the parent class. When the method is called, each device performs a different action depending on its object type.

2.
- The AbstractionDemo program demonstrates abstraction using smart home devices. It has an abstract class called SmartDevice and two child classes called SmartLight and SmartFan. The abstract class contains an abstract method called turnOn() and a regular method called showStatus(). The child classes provide their own implementation of the turnOn() method.

How Abstraction Works in the Code
Abstract Class: SmartDevice
Abstract Method: turnOn()
Child Classes: SmartLight and SmartFan
Method Implementation: Both child classes implement the turnOn() method with their own actions.
The SmartLight class turns on the light, while the SmartFan class turns on the fan.

3. Difference Between Polymorphism and Abstraction
- Polymorphism allows one method to have different behaviors depending on the object that uses it. Abstraction hides the implementation details and focuses on what an object can do instead of how it does it. In simple terms, polymorphism focuses on different behaviors, while abstraction focuses on hiding unnecessary details.

Part 2
1. Why is method overriding important in polymorphism? What happens if a child class does not override the parent's method?
- Method overriding is important because it allows child classes to have their own behavior while using the same method from the parent class. If a child class does not override the parent's method, it will simply use the inherited method from the parent class instead of having its own behavior.

2. Why can't we create an object directly from an abstract class? What is the purpose of this restriction?
- We cannot create an object directly from an abstract class because it may contain abstract methods that do not have complete implementations. This restriction ensures that child classes provide the required implementations before objects are created. It also helps organize and simplify the program.

3. If you need to add another child class to your polymorphism program, how would your existing code handle it?
- I can add another child class that extends the parent class and overrides the existing method. For example, I can add a SmartSpeaker class that extends Device and overrides the turnOn() method to display a message that the smart speaker is playing music. The existing parent class can still be used to refer to the new child object.

4. Can polymorphism and abstraction work together in a single program? Explain how and provide an example.
- Yes, polymorphism and abstraction can work together in a single program. Abstraction hides unnecessary details and provides a common structure, while polymorphism allows different child classes to perform different actions using the same method.
For example, an abstract class called Device can have an abstract method called performTask(). Its child classes, Television and Speaker, can implement the method differently. The television displays a movie, while the speaker plays music. Both use the same method but perform different actions.

5. As a future programmer, when would you use polymorphism, and when would you use abstraction?
- As a future programmer, I would use polymorphism when I want different objects to perform different actions using the same method. It helps make programs more flexible and easier to manage. I would use abstraction when I want to hide unnecessary implementation details and provide a common structure for related classes. It helps organize code and makes programs easier to understand and maintain.